===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[bell]] or [[chime]].
#* '''1997''', Sarah Gregory, ''Public Trust'', Signet (1997), {{ISBN|9780451190765}}, page 47:
#*: Sharon patted the '''dinger''' to call for service.
# {{lb|en|baseball}} A [[home run]].
#: ''The starting pitcher gave up three '''dingers'''.''
#* '''1989''', John Holway, "[http://books.google.com/books?id=ETQDAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA24 Strikeouts: The High Cost of Hitting Home Runs]", ''Baseball Digest'', June 1989:
#*: He should know, he fanned 2597 times — far more than any other man — but made millions hitting 563 '''dingers'''.
#* '''1997''', Hank Davis, ''Small-Town Heroes: Images of Minor League Baseball'', University of Nebraska Press (2003), {{ISBN|0803266391}}, [http://books.google.com/books?id=0T6QCyo7qrwC&pg=PA264 page 264]:
#*: Then as you're taking his picture, say something about the thirty '''dingers''' he's going to hit this season. You get that little extra smile on his face.
#* '''2008''', [[w:Mike Stone (radio personality)|Mike Stone]] & [[w:Art Regner|Art Regner]], ''The Great Book of Detroit Sports Lists'', Running Press (2008), {{ISBN|9780762433544}}, [http://books.google.com/books?id=aceROmDTQsoC&pg=PA209 page 209]:
#*: For you youngsters out there, hitting 50 '''dingers''' in the pre-steroid craze days of the early 90s was an actual accomplishment; the only questionable substance Fielder was putting in his body were McRib sandwiches.
# {{lb|en|North America|slang}} The [[penis]].
#* '''1994''', Max Evans, ''Bluefeather Fellini in the Sacred Realm'', University Press of Colorado (1994), {{ISBN|9780553565409}}, page 131:
#*: "He had a red wool sock on his '''dinger'''. That's all."
# {{lb|en|US|slang}} Something [[outstanding]] or [[exceptional]], a [[humdinger]].
#* '''1939''', {{w|John Steinbeck}}, ''{{w|The Grapes of Wrath}}'', Penguin, 1951, Chapter 4, p. 28,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/grapesofwrath1976stei]</sup>
#*: Casy said, “See how good the corn come along until the dust got up. Been a '''dinger''' of a crop.”
#* '''1998''', {{w|Earl Emerson}}, ''Catfish Café'', New York: Ballantine, Chapter 1, p. 3,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/catfishcafe00emer]</sup>
#*: “I won’t lie to you. She been in trouble the last couple years, but she got herself wrapped up in a real '''dinger''' this time.”
# {{lb|en|Australian slang|dated}} A [[condom]].
# {{lb|en|Australian slang}} The [[buttocks]], the [[anus]].
#: ''Let′s leave them to sit on their '''dingers''' for a while.''
#* '''1955''', Norman Bartlett, ''Island Victory'', Angus and Robertson (1955), [http://books.google.com/books?id=jVFEAAAAIAAJ&q=%22on+their+dingers%22 page 6]:
#*: "We'd get even more out of 'em if some of the pilots sat on their '''dingers''' less and polished their kites more."
#* '''1979''', Derek Maitland, ''Breaking Out'', Allen Lane (1979), [http://books.google.com/books?id=Vi9XAAAAYAAJ&q=%22his+dinger%22 page 63]:
#*: And why had he belted the Australian envoy flat on his '''dinger''' in that Spanish bar?
#* '''1988''', Peter Pinney, ''The Barbarians: A Soldier's New Guinea Diary'', University of Queensland Press (1988), {{ISBN|9780702221583}}, [http://books.google.com/books?id=rNtHAAAAYAAJ&q=%22his+dinger%22 page 109]:
#*: "Yeah? Well, stand up anyone who's got a three-inch mortar hid up his '''dinger'''!"
# {{lb|en|Australian slang}} A [[catapult]], a [[shanghai]].
#* '''2010''', [[w:Gordon Briscoe|Gordon Briscoe]], ''Racial Folly: A Twentieth-Century Aboriginal Family'', Anu E Press (2010), {{ISBN|9781921666209}}, [http://books.google.com/books?id=FH0MenEDMsIC&pg=PA59 page 59]:
#*: We made our ''''dingers'''' (as we called them) out of truck tyre inner tubes that were heavy-duty rubber that could shoot a stone a very long distance.

